  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 335 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonally warm temperatures and comfortable \nhumidity continue today.\n\nZonal flow aloft prevails early this morning with weak high \npressure at the surface. Mostly clear early this morning with \ntemperatures in the upper 50s to mid 60s across most of the \narea, and upper 60s/near 70 for coastal NE NC. Zonal flow aloft \nwill allow for the surface high to slide offshore. The low-level\nflow becomes S/SE, which will allow high temperatures to rise \ninto the mid/upper 80s (lower 80s at the coast). Dewpoints will \nincrease as well, but overall, humidity levels will remain quite\ntolerable for August.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled conditions return Thursday through \nSaturday with daily chances for showers and thunderstorms.\n\nGlobal models indicate that surface high pressure settles off \nthe Southeast coast later today into Thursday and the flow aloft\nshifts back to the SW. A slow moving front approaches the area \nThursday, but this boundary may remain NW of the local area \nbased on the consensus of the 00z/26 guidance. The 00z/26 HREF \nis rather unimpressive with respect to convective coverage and \nheavy rain threat. Therefore, PoPs have been nudged downward. \nThe severe threat seems low for Thursday at this time with only \n20-25kt of 500mb flow and relatively weak mid-level lapse rates.\nThe boundary drops SE Thursday night and settles into the \neastern Carolinas and SE VA by Friday, but remains rather \ndiffuse. There is a strengthening jet core over New England, but\nthere is some uncertainty as to how well the RRQ of this \nfeature interacts with the surface boundary. PoPs remain 50-70% \nFriday, but this could be a case of generous NBM PPI, and time \nwill tell how much coverage that CAMs have once we are their \nforecast time range. The main threat will be locally heavy rain \nfrom showers/tstms. WPC maintains a Marginal Risk of excessive \nrainfall Thursday and Friday, with a Slight Risk creeping into \nNE NC Friday , but given the setup, the showers/tstms seem \nscattered by nature causing a lower confidence in flooding. The \nconsensus amongst the 00z/26 models pushes the front S Saturday \nwith the best shower/tstm chances across SE VA/NE NC, then \ntrending drier Sunday into early next week with PoPs near to \nslightly below climo.\n\nSeasonally hot and humid Thursday through Sunday with high \ntemperatures primarily in the mid to upper 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Hot conditions return early next week.\n\nHot conditions return early next week as a 1.5 st dev 500mb \nridge builds from the Central Plains to the Southeast. NBM high \ntemperatures Monday through Wednesday are primarily in the upper\n80s to lower 80s, with locally mid 90s. However, 850mb \ntemperatures of 20-22C and 1000-850mb thickness schemes support \nhighs in the mid to upper 90s. If the hotter temperatures are \nrealized that will mean deeper mixing, which should allow \ndewpoints to drop into the upper 60s/around 70F during peak \nheating. Regardless, this would support heat indices into the \nlower 100s.",
